rection but it  was I think scary for me only because it  was a because Harry is the audience and  in a way in a big way I think and so if  if I screwed that up I thought maybe the  audience would would maybe stop  following this this this belief in Ruby  sparks so it was important we did a  bunch of different takes with the  laughing and being serious and being  scared of her and being amazed and  seeing a ghost and they put what what  I'm happy what they used and Kris makes  those two scenes seem very easy and you  know like of course that's the way you  would react but but trying to figure out  how one would react to a situation which  we never actually encounter in life is  such a challenge you made it so human  and so real and it was just such a gift  to the film and you know it also pulling  the yeah and and the physicality even in  the stuffed in the car was just so  wonderful so when you're dealing with a  fantastic subject matter and there's  certainly fantastic elements in this  movie it's really important in my  opinion to ground it in a recognizable  reality so the audience is immediately  feeling like this is the world that we  live in that this is taking place in and  and it doesn't it think about it and  then they're thinking about the story  and the characters and beyond that there  were things that we wanted to do  specifically to create the reality of  these characters Calvin was a young  writer he had a lot of money at one  point that he got from writing his first  book and he probably lived in a big  house what kind of house was this where  was it we ended up shooting almost  everything in the silver lake in las  filas area which seemed like the kind of  neighborhood he would live in and but  then there were considerations beyond  that we wanted a space that would allow  the actor  to move in a certain way and to develop  the characters in a certain way so all  of those elements came into  consideration when we the first thing we  did was try to 